public static int squarePermutations(int[] A) {
    int n = A.length;
    int[] prefixSums = new int[n + 1];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        prefixSums[i + 1] = prefixSums[i] + A[i];
    }

    int result = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j <= n; j++) {
            int sum = prefixSums[j] - prefixSums[i];
            if (isPerfectSquare(sum)) {
                result++;
            }
        }
    }

    return result;
}

private static boolean isPerfectSquare(int num) {
    double sqrt = Math.sqrt(num);
    return sqrt % 1 == 0;
}
```

Note that this is a brute force approach, and the time complexity is O(n^2) where n is the length of the input array. A more efficient solution would be to use a hash table to store the perfect squares and their count, and then use this count to calculate the number of square permutations.

Also, note that the input array contains non-negative integers only, which means that the sum of any pair of adjacent elements in the array will be a non-negative integer. Therefore, we don't need to check for negative integers in the `isPerfectSquare` function.

